description:
## Overview

Discovered while implementing real DWCP benchmarks for novacron-38p Phase 0 (Step 2: migration-time benchmark composing HDE+AMST primitives directly, rather than routing through MigrationAdapter).

`MigrationAdapter.receiveMemory` and `MigrationAdapter.receiveDisk` (`backend/core/network/dwcp/migration_adapter.go:743-752`) are empty stubs:

```go
// receiveMemory receives memory data from a migration
func (adapter *MigrationAdapter) receiveMemory(conn net.Conn) {
	// Implementation would receive and decompress memory data
	// This is a placeholder for the actual implementation
}

// receiveDisk receives disk data from a migration
func (adapter *MigrationAdapter) receiveDisk(conn net.Conn) {
	// Implementation would receive and decompress disk data
	// This is a placeholder for the actual implementation
}
```

`handleIncomingMigration` (line 723) reads one type byte off the connection, dispatches to one of these two no-ops, then `defer conn.Close()` fires immediately. `ListenForMigrations`/`handleIncomingMigration` accept and immediately close connections without decompressing or applying any received data — meaning `MigrationAdapter`'s server side has never been functional for either memory or disk migration.

## Why this matters

Any real migration attempted through `MigrationAdapter.MigrateVMMemory` + `ListenForMigrations` end-to-end would hit broken-pipe/silent-data-loss behavior on the receiving side — the sender transfers real bytes, the receiver reads a type byte and drops the connection. This was ruled out as a benchmark path for exactly this reason: the novacron-38p migration-time benchmark (`benchmark_migration_time_test.go`) instead composes `HDE.CompressMemory` + `AMST.Transfer` directly into a real draining loopback sink, deliberately bypassing `MigrationAdapter`, so it could only ever measure the compress+transfer primitives a migration would use — never production end-to-end migration time.

## Fix scope

Real implementation needed: `receiveMemory`/`receiveDisk` need to read the AMST-framed chunks off `conn` (matching `AMST.Transfer`'s wire format: 16-byte header of chunk id/offset/length/total, then chunk data — see `amst.go:381-386`), reassemble the full payload, and (for compressed payloads) decompress via `HDE.Decompress`/equivalent baseline-aware reconstruction before applying to the target VM. Nontrivial — needs product/domain decisions on how the receiver correlates an incoming stream with a VM/migration ID (the current wire format has no such envelope), which is out of scope for a bug report.

## Priority

P2, not blocking — novacron-38p's benchmarks work around it by testing the real primitives directly. But the production migration-adapter feature itself needs real implementation before `MigrationAdapter` can be used end-to-end.
